  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Visualizing Abstract Concepts**\n\nI\'m currently focused on translating the abstract concept into a vivid visual, playing with the idea of interchangeable colored and white elements. The reference images, particularly the \"Steamy Glass Overlays,\" provide a tactile element I\'m keen to incorporate. I\'m exploring how to abstract that feeling into a more conceptual representation.\n\n\n**Refining Visual Metaphors**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the \"frosted glass texture\" as a core element. The reference images, especially the glowing glass card and the \"Atlas Lens\" dashboard, are helping me explore how to translate this into a visual metaphor. The goal is to capture that sense of transparency, light refraction, and subtle detail, perhaps incorporating the prismatic effects and edge highlights. I\'m also considering the integration of a realistic glass thickness and inner shadows.\n\n\n**Developing Visual Themes**\n\nI\'m now iterating on a specific user interface concept. I envision glass slabs with prismatic rainbow light caustics along the beveled edges. I\'m focusing on a vertical glass pill bar on the left with icons, and a profile card. Middle cards would feature note statistics, a calendar, and recently opened items. The bottom row will include weather, AI insights, directories, to-dos, and news. A horizontal floating glass pill bar will host quick actions. Finally, giant 3D transparent glass typography will grace the top, with switchable colorful and white modes.\n\n\n**Defining Visual Parameters**\n\nMy focus has shifted to explicitly defining the visual parameters. I\'m moving away from \"AI slop\" and prioritizing the elements of crystalline liquid glass, glassmorphism, and prismatic glass tiles with realistic refractive borders and caustics. The key examples I\'m drawing from are image-1790114036816.png and Gemini_Generated_Image_1gghpj1gghpj1ggh.jpg, and the goal is to integrate water droplet and misty mountain backgrounds. I\'m imagining a switcher between \"Weiss\" (crystal frost, pure white ice glass) and \"Bunt\" (prismatic hologram, vibrant glass, sunset neon).\n\n\n**Structuring Interface Elements**\n\nI\'m now concretizing the user interface. I\'m building a vertical glass sidebar on the left, which will house icons for key functions. The main area will present glass widgets, directly modeled from those images, e.g., for Obsidian status, note statistics, calendar, quick retrieval, to-dos (including integrating Blake Crosley\'s guide), and AI insights. I\'m also planning interactive elements, such as a date picker and a reciprocal rank fusion calculator. Finally, I\'m integrating a personal touch and a mode toggle (\"Weiss\" \342\207\204 \"Bunt\").\n\n\n**Detailing Specific Interface Elements**\n\nI\'m now detailing the specific glass interface elements. I\'m focusing on the \"Verzeichnis & .indexignore\" directory view, the \"Wetter & System-Status\" (weather and system status) widget, both utilizing advanced search technologies. Also, the bottom floating glass pill bar hosts quick actions like \"Neue Notiz\" and \"Modus wechseln (Bunt / Weiss)\".\n\nI\'m carefully refining the styling details. I envision a realistic alpine mountain lake backdrop with reflections and subtle steam. The \"Weiss\" mode features daylight alpine snow mountains, frosty blue water, and crisp white glass cards, while \"Bunt\" mode presents a sunset/twilight scene with glowing holographic glass cards and vibrant neon refractions. I\'m aiming for crisp typography across both modes.\n\n\n**Implementing Glassmorphism Details**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into the CSS, aiming for realistic 3D glassmorphism. I\'m focusing on `backdrop-blur` effects, layered gradients for vibrant borders with rainbow caustics (Weiss mode), and electric cyan/magenta glow (Bunt mode). I\'m also adding inner reflections and embossing effects to 3D glass titles, and considering water droplet and condensation cues. I\'m reviewing the existing project files and installed packages to ensure compatibility.\n\n\n"
