  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Modal Implementations**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into the modal implementations. I\'m focusing on five specific modals: `GlassChecklistModal.tsx`, `GlassSimulatorModal.tsx`, `GlassNoteModal.tsx`, `GlassGraphModal.tsx`, and `GlassAudioPlayerModal.tsx`. A common theme I\'ve noticed across all of them is an early return if the modal isn\'t open; specifically, `if (!isOpen) return null;` is ubiquitous.\n\n\n**Refining Modal Rendering**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on the conditional rendering of modals and its implications. Returning null early if `isOpen` is false before hook execution is problematic. React 18/19 throws an error when hooks are present after an initial return. Best practice now involves keeping the modal conditionally mounted with `{isSomethingOpen && <Modal ... />}` in `App.tsx` and all hooks defined unconditionally at the top of each modal.\n\n\n**Optimizing Modal Logic**\n\nI see a clear pattern emerging in `GlassChecklistModal.tsx`. The early return if the modal isn\'t open is still present. However, if the hooks are moved above the conditional return, we can avoid potential React errors. I\'m noting this specifically for `GlassChecklistModal.tsx` now. The same pattern is present in `GlassSimulatorModal.tsx` as well.\n\n\n**Examining Modal Consistency**\n\nI\'m now back at examining `GlassGraphModal.tsx` and `GlassNoteModal.tsx`. The early returns before hook calls are still present in these modals, too. Specifically, `if (!isOpen) return null;` on line 64 of `GlassGraphModal.tsx` and lines 27 in `GlassNoteModal.tsx`. I see that the same issue exists. Hooks should be called unconditionally!\n\n\n**Identifying Modal Antipatterns**\n\nI\'m seeing a consistent antipattern in `GlassNoteModal.tsx` and `GlassAudioPlayerModal.tsx`. Both contain early returns before hook calls. Specifically, the checks `if (!isOpen || !note) return null;` in the note modal and `if (!isOpen) return null;` in the audio player modal. This is a recurring issue. My next step will be to explore other potential modal logic issues.\n\n\n**Integrating Hooks Correctly**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on hook integration, specifically `useRef` and `useEffect`. I\'m seeing this pattern repeated, especially in `App.tsx`. The conditional rendering using `&&` is working well, keeping all hooks at the top. This avoids errors and improves modal management.\n\n\n"
